/* ===== Module 9 ===== */
COURSE.push({
id: 'm9', short: 'API and network', title: 'API testing and network control', minutes: 65,
lead: 'How to talk to the server directly, how to wait for what the page asks for, and how to answer the page with data that you choose.',
learn: ['call an API from a test and check the response', 'use an API for fast set-up and for checking what a UI action really did', 'wait for a response that the page triggers', 'mock a response, and say when mocking is a good idea and when it is not'],
body: `
<h2>Why a UI tool also talks to the server</h2>
<p>A page is only the surface. Behind it, the page sends requests to a server and shows the answers. Playwright can take part at both levels, and mixing them is often the smartest move.</p>
${tbl(['Use of the API in a test', 'Example'], [
  ['<b>Set up data quickly</b>', 'Create a user and two accounts with API calls in 100 ms, instead of clicking through three forms'],
  ['<b>Check what the UI really did</b>', 'After a transfer in the page, ask the server for the balances, because the page may show something that the database does not have'],
  ['<b>Test the API itself</b>', 'Check the status and the fields of a response, with no browser page at all']
])}
<h2>Calling an API</h2>
<p>Playwright has its own HTTP client, called the <b>request context</b>. You create one with a base address, and then send <code>get</code>, <code>post</code> and so on. A response can be checked with <code>to_be_ok</code> (a status in the 200s) and read as JSON. The API check on the first line below is a normal web-first one.</p>
${code({ py: 'api = playwright.request.new_context(base_url="https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com")\n\nresponse = api.get("/todos/1")\n\nexpect(response).to_be_ok()\nassert response.json()["id"] == 1\napi.dispose()', kt: 'val api = playwright.request().newContext(\n    APIRequest.NewContextOptions().setBaseURL("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com")\n)\n\nval response = api.get("/todos/1")\n\nassertThat(response).isOK()\nassertEquals(1, JsonParser.parseString(response.text()).asJsonObject["id"].asInt)\napi.dispose()', file: { py: 'An API check', kt: 'An API check' }, ex: 'api-network', part: true })}
<p>Reading a JSON value with a plain assert is fine here: an API answer is not a page that changes while you look at it.</p>
${co('A small trap with base addresses', 'If the base address has a path, such as <code>https://host/app</code>, then a request to <code>"/orders"</code> goes to <code>https://host/orders</code>, because a leading slash starts again from the host. End the base address with a slash (<code>https://host/app/</code>) and write the path <b>without</b> a leading slash (<code>"orders"</code>). This is how the final project in module 12 reaches its server.', true)}
<h2>What the page does behind the scenes</h2>
<p>When a page loads a list, it usually sends a request (for example <code>GET /api/v1/fruits</code>) and then draws the answer. Playwright can see these requests, wait for them, and even answer them itself.</p>
<h3>Wait for a response</h3>
<p>Sometimes the thing to check is not on the screen yet, but in the request. The test says: "start waiting for this response, then do the thing that triggers it".</p>
${code({ py: 'with page.expect_response("**/api/v1/fruits") as response_info:\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n\nassert response_info.value.status == 200', kt: 'val response = page.waitForResponse("**/api/v1/fruits") {\n    page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n}\n\nassertEquals(200, response.status())', file: { py: 'Waiting for a response', kt: 'Waiting for a response' }, ex: 'api-network', part: true })}
<p>The pattern <code>**/api/v1/fruits</code> is a glob: <code>**</code> means "anything", so it matches the address on any host. The important order is: start waiting first, then trigger. If you click first and wait after, the response may already have gone by.</p>
<h3>Answer the page yourself: mocking</h3>
<p>A <b>route</b> catches requests that match a pattern. Your handler decides what happens: let it go to the server, answer it with your own data (<b>fulfill</b>), or cut it off (<b>abort</b>). Register the route <b>before</b> the page makes the request.</p>
${code({ py: 'page.route(\n    "**/api/v1/fruits",\n    lambda route: route.fulfill(json=[{"name": "Dragon fruit", "id": 99}]),\n)\n\npage.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n\nexpect(page.get_by_text("Dragon fruit")).to_be_visible()\nexpect(page.get_by_text("Strawberry")).to_have_count(0)', kt: 'page.route("**/api/v1/fruits") { route ->\n    route.fulfill(\n        Route.FulfillOptions()\n            .setContentType("application/json")\n            .setBody("""[{"name": "Dragon fruit", "id": 99}]""")\n    )\n}\n\npage.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n\nassertThat(page.getByText("Dragon fruit")).isVisible()\nassertThat(page.getByText("Strawberry")).hasCount(0)', file: { py: 'Mocking a response', kt: 'Mocking a response' }, ex: 'api-network', part: true })}
{{w:network}}
<h2>When to mock, and when not</h2>
${tbl(['Mock it when', 'Keep it real when'], [
  ['You need a state that is hard to cause: an error, an empty list, a very long list, a slow answer', 'You are testing that the page and the server really work together'],
  ['A third-party service is slow, costs money or is not available in tests', 'The test is one of the few end-to-end journeys that prove the whole system'],
  ['You want the test to be fast and the same on every run', 'The contract between page and server changes often: a mock would keep passing while the real thing breaks']
])}
<p>A healthy suite mixes both: many fast tests with mocked data for the states of the page, and a few real ones for the journeys.</p>
<h2>Step by step: API, waiting and mocking on real pages</h2>
${steps('m9', [
  { do: 'Add the file with three tests: an API check, a mocked page, and a wait for the real response.', code: code({ py: 'from playwright.sync_api import Page, Playwright, expect\n\n\ndef test_api_returns_a_todo(playwright: Playwright):\n    api = playwright.request.new_context(base_url="https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com")\n\n    response = api.get("/todos/1")\n\n    expect(response).to_be_ok()\n    assert response.json()["id"] == 1\n    api.dispose()\n\n\ndef test_shows_a_mocked_fruit(page: Page):\n    page.route(\n        "**/api/v1/fruits",\n        lambda route: route.fulfill(json=[{"name": "Dragon fruit", "id": 99}]),\n    )\n\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n\n    expect(page.get_by_text("Dragon fruit")).to_be_visible()\n    expect(page.get_by_text("Strawberry")).to_have_count(0)\n\n\ndef test_waits_for_the_real_response(page: Page):\n    with page.expect_response("**/api/v1/fruits") as response_info:\n        page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n\n    assert response_info.value.status == 200', kt: 'package course\n\nimport com.google.gson.JsonParser\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.APIRequest\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.Route\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.assertions.PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test\n\nclass ApiAndNetworkTest : BaseTest() {\n    @Test\n    fun apiReturnsATodo() {\n        val api = playwright.request().newContext(\n            APIRequest.NewContextOptions().setBaseURL("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com")\n        )\n\n        val response = api.get("/todos/1")\n\n        assertThat(response).isOK()\n        assertEquals(1, JsonParser.parseString(response.text()).asJsonObject["id"].asInt)\n        api.dispose()\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun showsAMockedFruit() {\n        page.route("**/api/v1/fruits") { route ->\n            route.fulfill(\n                Route.FulfillOptions()\n                    .setContentType("application/json")\n                    .setBody("""[{"name": "Dragon fruit", "id": 99}]""")\n            )\n        }\n\n        page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n\n        assertThat(page.getByText("Dragon fruit")).isVisible()\n        assertThat(page.getByText("Strawberry")).hasCount(0)\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun waitsForTheRealResponse() {\n        val response = page.waitForResponse("**/api/v1/fruits") {\n            page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n        }\n\n        assertEquals(200, response.status())\n    }\n}', file: { py: 'tests/test_api_and_network.py', kt: 'src/test/kotlin/course/ApiAndNetworkTest.kt' }, ex: 'api-network' }), why: L('The <code>playwright</code> fixture is the entry point of the library, and its <code>request</code> property creates request contexts.', 'In Kotlin, <code>playwright</code> is the field of your <code>BaseTest</code>, and Gson (used for the JSON) arrives with the Playwright library, so no extra dependency is needed.'), see: 'Nothing yet.' },
  { do: 'Run the file.', code: sh({ py: 'pytest tests/test_api_and_network.py', kt: './gradlew test --tests "course.ApiAndNetworkTest"' }), why: 'Three different tools in one run: an API call, a mock and a wait for a real response.', see: L('<code>3 passed</code>', 'Three lines that end with <code>PASSED</code>') },
  { do: 'Change the mocked fruit to your own name, and add a second fruit to the list. Run again and look at the page picture in a headed run.', why: 'You are the server now. Changing the data and seeing the page follow is the best way to feel what a mock does.', see: 'Both of your fruits are on the page, and the real fruits are gone.' },
  { do: 'Make the mock fail: answer with status 500 instead of data, and look at what the page shows.', code: code({ py: 'lambda route: route.fulfill(status=500, body="boom")', kt: 'route.fulfill(Route.FulfillOptions().setStatus(500).setBody("boom"))', label: 'Instead of the json answer' }), why: 'This is the main reason to mock: states that are hard to cause on a real server. What the page does with a failing server is exactly what a user would see on a bad day.', see: 'No fruit list. Whether that is acceptable (a message, a blank page) is a question for the product, and now you can ask it with evidence.' }
])}
`,
takeaways: ['A request context lets a test call an API directly, for fast data set-up, for checks behind the UI, and for API tests.', 'Start waiting for a response before the action that triggers it.', 'A route catches requests: fulfill answers with your data, abort cuts the request. Register it before the page asks.', 'Mock for states that are hard to cause and for third parties. Keep a few real end-to-end tests.', 'A base address with a path ends with a slash, and the paths after it have no leading slash.'],
quiz: [
  { q: 'When is a direct API call better than clicking through the page?', o: ['To create test data quickly, when the form itself is not what you test', 'Always, because pages are not worth testing', 'Never, because tests must only use the page', 'Only when the page is down'], a: 0, why: 'An API call creates data in milliseconds. Use the form only in the test that is about the form.' },
  { type: 'multi', q: 'Which of these are good uses of Playwright\'s API testing?', o: ['Setting up data before a UI test', 'Checking through the API what a UI action really did', 'Testing the API itself, with no page', 'Replacing every UI test with API calls', 'Drawing the pages in the browser'], a: [0, 1, 2], why: 'Mixing API and UI is the strength. Dropping all UI tests would leave the page itself unchecked.' },
  { q: 'What does page.route do?', o: ['It catches requests that match a pattern, so that the test can answer, change or cancel them', 'It chooses the fastest route through the page', 'It opens a new tab', 'It records a video'], a: 0, why: 'A route sits between the page and the network, and your handler decides what happens to each matching request.' },
  { q: 'What is a risk of mocking every response?', o: ['The tests keep passing while the real server has changed, so a real problem stays hidden', 'Mocks are slower than a real server', 'Mocks cannot return JSON', 'The browser refuses mocked data'], a: 0, why: 'A mock is a promise about what the server says. If the server changes, only a real test notices.' },
  { type: 'fill', q: L('Which method of the route object answers a request with data that you supply? (name only)', 'Which method of the route object answers a request with data that you supply? (name only)'), ans: ['fulfill', 'route.fulfill'], why: 'fulfill answers with your status and body. abort cuts the request. fetch lets it go to the server, so that you can change the answer.' },
  { q: 'The base address is https://host/app and your request path is "/orders". Where does the request go?', o: ['https://host/orders, because a leading slash starts again from the host', 'https://host/app/orders', 'https://host/app//orders', 'It is an error'], a: 0, why: 'End the base address with a slash and write the path without a leading slash to stay inside /app/.' },
  { type: 'order', q: 'Put the steps of a mocking test in order.', items: ['Register the route for the request', 'Make the page send the request (open it or click)', 'The route handler answers with your data', 'Assert on what the page shows'], why: 'The route must exist before the request is made, or the real server will answer.' }
],
practice: { id: 'p-m9', title: 'API, wait and mock',
  brief: '<p>In your project, using the fruit demo page, write <b>two</b> tests. (1) A mocked list with two fruits of your choice: assert that both are visible and that "Strawberry" is not on the page. (2) A test with the real server: wait for the response of the fruits request, check that its status is 200 and that the JSON list has at least one item with a <code>name</code>. Then add a third test that calls <code>https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1</code> and checks the <code>title</code> field.</p>',
  template: { py: '# 1. mocked list\n\n# 2. real response\n\n# 3. API call\n', kt: '// 1. mocked list\n\n// 2. real response\n\n// 3. API call\n' },
  checks: ['The route is registered before the page is opened', 'The mocked test asserts both what is shown and what is not', 'The real-response test starts waiting before the navigation', 'The API test checks the status and a field of the JSON', 'No test uses a sleep'],
  model: code({ py: 'def test_two_mocked_fruits(page: Page):\n    page.route(\n        "**/api/v1/fruits",\n        lambda route: route.fulfill(\n            json=[{"name": "Lychee", "id": 1}, {"name": "Mango", "id": 2}]\n        ),\n    )\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n    expect(page.get_by_text("Lychee")).to_be_visible()\n    expect(page.get_by_text("Mango")).to_be_visible()\n    expect(page.get_by_text("Strawberry")).to_have_count(0)\n\n\ndef test_real_response_has_fruits(page: Page):\n    with page.expect_response("**/api/v1/fruits") as info:\n        page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n    assert info.value.status == 200\n    assert "name" in info.value.json()[0]\n\n\ndef test_todo_title(playwright: Playwright):\n    api = playwright.request.new_context(base_url="https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com")\n    response = api.get("/todos/1")\n    expect(response).to_be_ok()\n    assert response.json()["title"] == "delectus aut autem"\n    api.dispose()', kt: '@Test\nfun twoMockedFruits() {\n    page.route("**/api/v1/fruits") { route ->\n        route.fulfill(\n            Route.FulfillOptions()\n                .setContentType("application/json")\n                .setBody("""[{"name": "Lychee", "id": 1}, {"name": "Mango", "id": 2}]""")\n        )\n    }\n    page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n    assertThat(page.getByText("Lychee")).isVisible()\n    assertThat(page.getByText("Mango")).isVisible()\n    assertThat(page.getByText("Strawberry")).hasCount(0)\n}\n\n@Test\nfun realResponseHasFruits() {\n    val response = page.waitForResponse("**/api/v1/fruits") {\n        page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking/")\n    }\n    assertEquals(200, response.status())\n    assertTrue(JsonParser.parseString(response.text()).asJsonArray[0].asJsonObject.has("name"))\n}\n\n@Test\nfun todoTitle() {\n    val api = playwright.request().newContext(\n        APIRequest.NewContextOptions().setBaseURL("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com")\n    )\n    val response = api.get("/todos/1")\n    assertThat(response).isOK()\n    assertEquals("delectus aut autem", JsonParser.parseString(response.text()).asJsonObject["title"].asString)\n    api.dispose()\n}', file: { py: 'Reference solution', kt: 'Reference solution' } }) + '<p>The third test uses a plain assert for the title because an API answer does not change while you look at it. The first two use web-first assertions on the page. In both of the page tests, the route or the wait is set up <b>before</b> the navigation.</p>' }
});

/* ===== Module 10 ===== */
COURSE.push({
id: 'm10', short: 'Debugging and flaky tests', title: 'Debugging and flaky tests', minutes: 65,
lead: 'How to find out why a test failed without guessing, which tools show you what happened, and how to deal with tests that sometimes pass and sometimes fail.',
learn: ['record and open a trace, and read the steps and the page at the moment of failure', 'use the headed mode, screenshots and video to see what the test saw', 'name the usual causes of a flaky test and match symptoms to causes', 'measure how flaky a test is, and decide what to do about it'],
body: `
<h2>Do not guess: look</h2>
<p>When a test fails, the temptation is to change something and run it again. That is slow and it teaches nothing. Playwright is good at recording what it did, so the better habit is: <b>get the evidence, then decide</b>. The tools below go from the quickest to the most complete.</p>
${tbl(['Tool', 'What it gives you', 'Python', 'Kotlin'], [
  ['Headed and slow motion', 'You watch the browser', '<code>--headed --slowmo 700</code>', 'Launch with <code>setHeadless(false).setSlowMo(700.0)</code>'],
  ['Inspector and pause', 'Stops the test so you can step, inspect and try locators', '<code>PWDEBUG=1 pytest</code> or a <code>page.pause()</code> line', 'Environment variable <code>PWDEBUG=1</code> or a <code>page.pause()</code> line'],
  ['<b>Trace</b>', 'Every step with the page before and after, network and console. The best tool for a failure you cannot watch, such as one on CI', '<code>--tracing retain-on-failure</code>', 'Tracing in the test class (see the steps below)'],
  ['Screenshot and video', 'A picture or a film of the failing test', '<code>--screenshot only-on-failure --video retain-on-failure</code>', 'Context option <code>setRecordVideoDir</code>, and <code>page.screenshot(...)</code>']
])}
<h2>The trace: a flight recorder</h2>
<p>An airplane records everything, so that after an incident the investigators can replay it. A <b>trace</b> does the same for a test. It is a zip file with a timeline of every action. For each step it keeps a snapshot of the page before and after, the exact locator, the network requests and the messages in the browser console. You open it in the trace viewer and click through the test as if it were a film that you can pause.</p>
${co('What to look for in a trace', '1) Find the <b>red</b> step: it is where the test stopped. 2) Look at the page picture at that moment: is the element there, hidden, or covered by something? 3) Read the error under the step and the call log: what was Playwright waiting for? 4) Check the network tab for a failed request, and the console tab for a JavaScript error. Most failures show themselves within a minute.', false)}
{{w:trace}}
<h2>What a flaky test is</h2>
<p>A <b>flaky</b> test passes and fails on the same code, with no change in between. It is worse than a plainly failing test, because the team learns to ignore red builds. There are only a few usual causes, and learning to name them is half of the cure.</p>
${tbl(['Cause', 'Typical symptom', 'Cure'], [
  ['A timing guess', 'Fails more on slow machines, after a sleep or a plain check', 'Wait for the condition with an action or a web-first assertion'],
  ['Tests that depend on each other', 'Passes alone, fails in the suite, or depends on the order', 'Independent tests, a fresh context and data in every test'],
  ['Data that collides', '"Already exists", or a count that is off by one', 'Unique data in every run'],
  ['The environment', 'Failures in bursts, timeouts talking to another service', 'Mock the unstable part, keep a few real tests'],
  ['A weak locator', 'Strict mode violations, or the wrong element chosen at times', 'Describe the element, not its position']
])}
{{w:flaky}}
<h2>Measure, then fix, then measure again</h2>
<p>You cannot fix what you cannot see. To find out how flaky a test is, <b>run it many times</b>: in Python with the <code>pytest-repeat</code> plugin (<code>pytest --count 20</code>), in Kotlin with JUnit's <code>@RepeatedTest(20)</code>. A test that fails 1 time in 20 is flaky, and 20 runs take a minute on your own computer.</p>
${code({ py: 'pytest tests/test_first.py --count 20', kt: '@RepeatedTest(20)\nfun addsATodoEveryTime() {\n    // the same body as a normal test\n}', file: { py: 'Run a test many times', kt: 'RepeatTest.kt, a repeated test' } })}
<p><b>Retries</b> (running a failed test again, automatically) can keep a build green, but they hide the problem. Use them as a temporary measure while you investigate, count how many tests needed one, and put every repeat offender on a list to fix. A test that you cannot fix soon should be <b>quarantined</b>: taken out of the blocking set, but kept and tracked, so that nobody forgets it.</p>
<h2>Practise: debug in the lab</h2>
{{lab:l10a}}
<h2>Step by step: record a trace and open it</h2>
${steps('m10', [
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Run a test with the trace switched on.', code: sh('pytest tests/test_first.py --tracing on'), why: 'A trace costs a little time, so by default it is off. In CI the usual choice is <code>retain-on-failure</code>: it records every test and keeps the file only if the test fails.', see: 'The test passes, and a folder <code>test-results</code> appears, with a <code>trace.zip</code> inside the folder of the test.' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Open the trace.', code: sh('playwright show-trace test-results/<folder-of-the-test>/trace.zip'), why: 'The trace viewer is a small application that opens the zip. Replace the folder name with the real one from your <code>test-results</code>. You can also keep the screenshots and videos next to it with the flags from the table.', see: 'A window with the timeline of actions on the left and the page snapshot on the right.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Add a test class that records a trace for every test and saves it to <code>build/traces</code>.', code: code({ kt: 'package course\n\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.Tracing\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.assertions.PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterEach\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.TestInfo\nimport java.nio.file.Paths\n\nclass TraceTest : BaseTest() {\n    @BeforeEach\n    fun startTracing() {\n        context.tracing().start(\n            Tracing.StartOptions().setScreenshots(true).setSnapshots(true).setSources(true)\n        )\n    }\n\n    @AfterEach\n    fun stopTracing(testInfo: TestInfo) {\n        val name = testInfo.testMethod.get().name\n        context.tracing().stop(Tracing.StopOptions().setPath(Paths.get("build/traces/$name.zip")))\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun addsATodoAndKeepsATrace() {\n        page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n        val newTodo = page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")\n        newTodo.fill("Buy milk")\n        newTodo.press("Enter")\n\n        assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-title")).hasText("Buy milk")\n    }\n}', file: { kt: 'src/test/kotlin/course/TraceTest.kt' }, ex: 'trace' }), why: 'The <code>@BeforeEach</code> of this class runs after the one in <code>BaseTest</code> (so the context exists), and its <code>@AfterEach</code> runs <b>before</b> the one in <code>BaseTest</code> (so the context is still open). That order is exactly what tracing needs.', see: 'Nothing yet.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Run it, then open the trace with the shortcut task.', code: sh('./gradlew test --tests "course.TraceTest"\n./gradlew playwright --args="show-trace build/traces/addsATodoAndKeepsATrace.zip"'), why: 'The first command records the trace. The second opens the viewer. In a real project you would save the trace only when a test fails.', see: 'A window with the timeline of actions on the left and the page snapshot on the right.' },
  { do: 'In the viewer, click through the steps: look at the page <b>before</b> and <b>after</b> an action, open the Network tab, and find the locator that Playwright used for a click.', why: 'The page picture is a real snapshot of the page that you can inspect, not a flat screenshot. Learning to read it is what makes debugging fast.', see: 'You can say, for every step, what the page looked like and what the test did.' },
  { do: 'Break the test on purpose (a wrong expected text), run it with the trace on, and open the trace of the failure.', why: 'Practise on a failure that you caused. The red step, the error under it and the page picture at that moment tell the story.', see: 'The failing step is red, and the page picture shows the actual text.' },
  { do: L('Measure flakiness: run the first test 20 times.', 'Measure flakiness: change <code>@Test</code> to <code>@RepeatedTest(20)</code> on one test and run it.'), code: sh({ py: 'pip install pytest-repeat\npytest tests/test_first.py --count 20', kt: './gradlew test --tests "course.RepeatTest"' }), why: 'A number is better than a feeling. A test that passes 20 of 20 times is probably fine. One that fails once or twice deserves attention.', see: L('<code>20 passed</code>', 'Five repetitions end with <code>PASSED</code>, or the number you chose.') }
])}
`,
takeaways: ['Get evidence before you change anything: a trace, a screenshot, a video, or a headed run.', 'A trace is a recording of the whole test with the page before and after every step. Read it from the red step.', 'Flaky tests have a few usual causes: timing guesses, dependence between tests, colliding data, the environment, and weak locators.', 'Measure flakiness by running a test many times. Retries hide a problem, so count them and fix the repeat offenders.', 'Quarantine a test you cannot fix soon, but keep it on a list.'],
quiz: [
  { q: 'What is the best first move when a test fails on CI and you cannot watch it?', o: ['Look at its trace, screenshot or video', 'Add a sleep and run it again', 'Delete the test', 'Raise all timeouts to 5 minutes'], a: 0, why: 'A recording answers the question with evidence. Guessing changes the test without understanding it.' },
  { type: 'multi', q: 'What does a trace contain?', o: ['Every action with the page before and after it', 'The network requests', 'The browser console messages', 'The salary of the tester', 'A copy of the production database'], a: [0, 1, 2], why: 'A trace is a recording of the browser session of the test: steps, snapshots, network and console.' },
  { q: 'A test passes alone and fails in the full suite. What is the most likely cause?', o: ['The tests share state or depend on each other', 'The monitor is too small', 'The browser is too old', 'The test has too many assertions'], a: 0, why: 'Independence is broken: another test leaves data or state that this test did not expect.' },
  { q: 'A test fails every second run with "username already exists". What is the cure?', o: ['Unique data in every run', 'A longer timeout', 'A retry', 'A faster computer'], a: 0, why: 'The data collides with the data of the previous run. A random part in the name removes the collision.' },
  { q: 'Why are automatic retries only a temporary measure?', o: ['They keep the build green while the real problem stays hidden', 'They make the browser slower', 'Playwright does not support them', 'They change the result of the test'], a: 0, why: 'A flaky test that retries to green is still flaky. Count them and fix them.' },
  { type: 'fill', q: L('Which flag records a trace only for tests that fail? (the value after --tracing)', 'In JUnit, which annotation runs one test several times so that you can measure flakiness? (with the @)'), ans: ['retain-on-failure', '@RepeatedTest', 'RepeatedTest'], why: L('retain-on-failure records every test and keeps the file only when the test fails, which is the usual choice for CI.', '@RepeatedTest(20) runs the same test 20 times. A test that fails once in 20 is flaky.') },
  { type: 'order', q: 'Put the work on a flaky test in order.', items: ['Measure how often it fails by running it many times', 'Find the cause with a trace and by matching the symptoms', 'Fix the cause (a wait, data, a locator, an isolation problem)', 'Run it many times again and compare'], why: 'Measure, understand, fix, and measure again. Without the second measurement you do not know that it worked.' }
],
practice: { id: 'p-m10', title: 'Investigate a failure',
  brief: '<p>Take one of your tests and make it fail on purpose in a way that you choose (a wrong locator, a wrong expected text, or a missing element). Record it with a trace (and with a screenshot or a video if you like). Then write an <b>investigation note</b> with four lines: what the error said in its first line, which step in the trace was red, what the page looked like at that step, and what the fix is. Fix it, run the test 20 times, and write down the result.</p>',
  template: 'First line of the error:\n\nThe red step in the trace:\n\nThe page at that moment:\n\nThe fix:\n\nResult of 20 runs:\n',
  checks: ['I recorded a trace of a failing test and opened it', 'I named the red step and described the page at that step', 'My fix changes the cause, not only the timeout', 'I ran the fixed test 20 times and wrote down the result'],
  model: '<p><b>An example note.</b> First line: <code>TimeoutError: Locator.click: Timeout 30000ms exceeded</code>. The red step: <code>click</code> on <code>get_by_role("button", name="Clear complete")</code>. The page at that moment: the button is called "Clear completed", and it is visible. The fix: the name in the locator was a typo, so I corrected it. Result: 20 runs, 20 passes.</p><p><b>What matters is the order:</b> the evidence (error, red step, page) comes first, and the fix is a consequence. If your fix was "a longer timeout", look again: a timeout almost never fixes a locator that matches nothing.</p>' }
});

/* ===== Module 11 ===== */
COURSE.push({
id: 'm11', short: 'Config, parallel, CI', title: 'Configuration, parallel runs and CI', minutes: 65,
lead: 'How to run the same test with many data sets, how to make a suite fast with parallel runs, and how to have a machine run it for you on every change.',
learn: ['turn one test into many with parameters', 'put the address of the site and other settings in one place', 'run tests in parallel and say what makes that safe', 'write a CI job that installs, runs and keeps the evidence of a failure'],
body: `
<h2>One test, many data sets</h2>
<p>If three todos need the same steps, you do not write three tests. You write one and give it three inputs. A <b>parameterized test</b> runs once per input, and each run is reported separately, so a failure names the exact input that broke.</p>
${code({ py: '@pytest.mark.parametrize("title", ["Buy milk", "Walk the dog", "Write tests"])\ndef test_adds_each_title(page: Page, title: str):\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    new_todo = page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?")\n    new_todo.fill(title)\n    new_todo.press("Enter")\n\n    expect(page.get_by_test_id("todo-title")).to_have_text(title)', kt: '@ParameterizedTest\n@ValueSource(strings = ["Buy milk", "Walk the dog", "Write tests"])\nfun addsEachTitle(title: String) {\n    page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    val newTodo = page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")\n    newTodo.fill(title)\n    newTodo.press("Enter")\n\n    assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-title")).hasText(title)\n}', file: { py: 'A parameterized test', kt: 'A parameterized test' }, ex: 'param', part: true })}
<h2>Settings in one place</h2>
<p>The address of the site, the browser, whether to show it: these are <b>settings</b>, and they change between your laptop, CI and a test server. Do not write them into every test. Put the address in one place, so that you can point the whole suite at another environment with one change.</p>
${tbl(['Setting', 'Python', 'Kotlin'], [
  ['The base address, so that tests write <code>"/todomvc/"</code>', '<code>pytest --base-url https://demo.playwright.dev</code>, or <code>base_url = ...</code> in <code>pytest.ini</code>', '<code>Browser.NewContextOptions().setBaseURL(...)</code> in the base class'],
  ['Which browser', '<code>--browser chromium</code>, and you can repeat it: <code>--browser chromium --browser firefox</code>', '<code>playwright.chromium()</code>, <code>playwright.firefox()</code>, <code>playwright.webkit()</code>'],
  ['Show the browser, slow it down', '<code>--headed</code>, <code>--slowmo 500</code>', '<code>LaunchOptions().setHeadless(false).setSlowMo(500.0)</code>'],
  ['Secrets and passwords', 'Environment variables', 'Environment variables']
])}
<p>Firefox and WebKit are good for checking that the page works everywhere, but they need to be installed first, and on some machines (for example Windows without the Visual C++ runtime, or a Linux server without graphics libraries) they need extra system libraries. Chromium is the sensible default for most tests.</p>
<h2>Parallel runs</h2>
<p>A suite of 200 tests that take 6 seconds each needs 20 minutes if they run one after another. Four at a time, the same suite needs 5 minutes. This is the payoff of the isolation you built in modules 6 to 8: independent tests, each with its own context, can run side by side without disturbing each other. If your tests share data or depend on order, parallel runs will make them fail, and that is a useful finding, not a bug in the tool.</p>
${tbl(['', 'Python', 'Kotlin'], [
  ['How', 'The <code>pytest-xdist</code> plugin: <code>pytest -n auto</code>, or <code>-n 4</code> for four workers', 'JUnit parallel execution, switched on in <code>src/test/resources/junit-platform.properties</code>'],
  ['Unit that runs in parallel', 'A test', 'A test class (each class has its own Playwright and browser, thanks to <code>PER_CLASS</code> in <code>BaseTest</code>)'],
  ['What to watch', 'Shared files and shared accounts', 'Shared files and shared accounts']
])}
<h2>A machine that runs the tests for you</h2>
<p><b>Continuous integration</b> (CI) means that every change is tested automatically on a clean machine, so that you hear about a break within minutes, and "it works on my laptop" stops being an argument. A CI job is a list of steps that the machine follows, in order. Build it, so that you understand what each step is for:</p>
{{w:pipeline}}
<p>Two details that bite beginners. On a clean Linux machine the browsers need system libraries, which is why the install step says <code>--with-deps</code>. And the step that keeps the evidence (the traces and the report) must run <b>even when the tests failed</b>, which is what <code>if: always()</code> does.</p>
<h2>Reports</h2>
<p>A pass or fail line is not a report. pytest can write a standard XML report (<code>pytest --junitxml=report.xml</code>) that CI tools can display, and many teams add a richer report such as Allure on top. Gradle writes a readable HTML report of every run by itself, in <code>build/reports/tests/test/index.html</code>. Whatever you use, keep the report and the traces as downloadable files of the CI run.</p>
<h2>Step by step: parameters, settings, parallel, CI</h2>
${steps('m11', [
  { do: 'Add the parameterized test.', code: code({ py: 'import pytest\nfrom playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize("title", ["Buy milk", "Walk the dog", "Write tests"])\ndef test_adds_each_title(page: Page, title: str):\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    new_todo = page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?")\n    new_todo.fill(title)\n    new_todo.press("Enter")\n\n    expect(page.get_by_test_id("todo-title")).to_have_text(title)', kt: 'package course\n\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.assertions.PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat\nimport org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest\nimport org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.ValueSource\n\nclass ParametrizedTest : BaseTest() {\n    @ParameterizedTest\n    @ValueSource(strings = ["Buy milk", "Walk the dog", "Write tests"])\n    fun addsEachTitle(title: String) {\n        page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n        val newTodo = page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")\n        newTodo.fill(title)\n        newTodo.press("Enter")\n\n        assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-title")).hasText(title)\n    }\n}', file: { py: 'tests/test_parametrized.py', kt: 'src/test/kotlin/course/ParametrizedTest.kt' }, ex: 'param-file' }), why: 'One function, three runs. In the report each run has its own name with the input in it, so you see at once which input failed. In Kotlin, parameterized tests come with the <code>junit-jupiter</code> dependency that you already have.', see: L('<code>3 passed</code> after <code>pytest tests/test_parametrized.py</code>', 'Three lines, one for each title, after <code>./gradlew test --tests "course.ParametrizedTest"</code>') },
  { do: 'Move the address into the settings: add the base address test.', code: code({ py: 'from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_opens_the_app_from_the_base_url(page: Page):\n    page.goto("/todomvc/")\n\n    expect(page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?")).to_be_visible()', kt: 'package course\n\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.Browser\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.assertions.PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test\n\nclass BaseUrlTest : BaseTest() {\n    @Test\n    fun opensTheAppFromTheBaseUrl() {\n        val context = browser.newContext(Browser.NewContextOptions().setBaseURL("https://demo.playwright.dev"))\n        val page = context.newPage()\n\n        page.navigate("/todomvc/")\n\n        assertThat(page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")).isVisible()\n        context.close()\n    }\n}', file: { py: 'tests/test_base_url.py', kt: 'src/test/kotlin/course/BaseUrlTest.kt' }, ex: 'baseurl' }), why: 'The test says where it goes ("/todomvc/") but not on which server. The server is a setting.', see: L('Run <code>pytest tests/test_base_url.py --base-url https://demo.playwright.dev</code> and see <code>1 passed</code>. Without the flag it fails, because there is no base address.', '<code>BaseUrlTest > opensTheAppFromTheBaseUrl() PASSED</code>') },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Run the whole suite in parallel.', code: sh('pip install pytest-xdist\npytest -n 4 --base-url https://demo.playwright.dev'), why: 'Four workers each take tests from the queue. The time shrinks, and any hidden dependence between tests comes to light.', see: 'The same tests pass in less time. With 17 tests, four workers took under 4 seconds in my run.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Switch on parallel execution: create <code>src/test/resources/junit-platform.properties</code> and run the suite again.', code: code({ kt: 'junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled = true\njunit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.default = same_thread\njunit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.classes.default = concurrent', file: { kt: 'src/test/resources/junit-platform.properties' } }), why: 'Test classes now run at the same time, and the methods of one class run one after another. Each class has its own Playwright and browser, so nothing is shared between threads.', see: 'The same tests pass, in less time. The first run after a clean may be slower while the browsers are being used by several classes at once.' },
  { do: 'Ask for a report.', code: sh({ py: 'pytest --junitxml=report.xml', kt: './gradlew test\n# then open build/reports/tests/test/index.html in a browser' }), why: 'A report is the output of a run in a form that tools and people can open later, not only a line in a terminal.', see: L('A file <code>report.xml</code> with one entry per test.', 'An HTML page with the tests, the times and the failures.') },
  { do: 'Add the CI job. Create the file <code>.github/workflows/tests.yml</code> in your repository.', code: code({ py: 'name: Tests\non:\n  push:\n  pull_request:\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v7\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v7\n        with:\n          python-version: "3.12"\n          cache: pip\n      - run: pip install -r requirements.txt\n      - run: playwright install --with-deps chromium\n      - run: pytest --tracing retain-on-failure\n      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v7\n        if: always()\n        with:\n          name: test-results\n          path: test-results/\n          if-no-files-found: ignore', kt: 'name: Tests\non:\n  push:\n  pull_request:\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v7\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@v6\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: "21"\n      - uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@v6\n      - run: chmod +x gradlew\n      - run: ./gradlew playwright --args="install --with-deps chromium"\n      - run: ./gradlew test\n      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v7\n        if: always()\n        with:\n          name: test-report\n          path: |\n            build/reports/tests/test\n            build/traces\n          if-no-files-found: ignore', file: { py: '.github/workflows/tests.yml', kt: '.github/workflows/tests.yml' } }), why: L('Each step is one decision: get the code, get Python, install the libraries, install the browser with its system libraries, run the tests with a trace for failures, and keep the evidence even when the tests failed. If your project sits in a subfolder, add a <code>defaults: run: working-directory:</code> block to the job.', 'Each step is one decision: get the code, get Java, get Gradle, install the browser with its system libraries, run the tests, and keep the report and the traces even when the tests failed. The <code>chmod</code> line is there because a <code>gradlew</code> file created on Windows loses its "executable" flag, which makes Linux refuse to run it. If your project sits in a subfolder, add a <code>defaults: run: working-directory:</code> block to the job.'), see: 'After you push, the tab <b>Actions</b> of the repository shows the run. A green tick means that your tests passed on a clean machine. Open a failed run to download the artifact.' }
])}
`,
takeaways: ['A parameterized test runs once per input, and each run is reported by itself.', 'The address of the site and other settings live in one place, so one change points the suite at another environment.', 'Parallel runs cut the time, and they are safe when every test is independent and has its own context and data.', 'A CI job is an ordered list of steps: code, language, libraries, browsers with their system libraries, tests, evidence.', 'Keep the report and the traces as files of the CI run, and upload them even when the tests fail.'],
quiz: [
  { q: 'What is the benefit of a parameterized test?', o: ['One test body runs for many inputs, and each run is reported separately', 'It makes tests run slower on purpose', 'It removes the need for assertions', 'It works only with numbers'], a: 0, why: 'You write the steps once. A failure names the input that broke.' },
  { q: 'Why put the base address of the site in a setting?', o: ['One change points the whole suite at another environment', 'It makes the browser faster', 'Locators need it', 'Tests cannot contain addresses'], a: 0, why: 'The same tests can then run against a local build, a test server and a staging server.' },
  { q: 'What must be true for tests to run safely in parallel?', o: ['They are independent: no shared accounts, files or order', 'They all use one shared page', 'They are all very short', 'They run in the same browser tab'], a: 0, why: 'Parallel runs expose shared state immediately. Independent tests do not notice each other.' },
  { type: 'num', q: 'A suite has 200 tests of 6 seconds each. With 4 parallel workers, and ignoring any overhead, how many minutes does it take?', ans: 5, tol: 0.01, why: '200 x 6 = 1200 seconds. Divided by 4 workers: 300 seconds, which is 5 minutes (instead of 20).' },
  { q: 'Why does the CI step that uploads the traces usually say if: always()?', o: ['So that it runs even when the tests fail, which is when you need the traces', 'Because uploads are free', 'Because the test step cannot fail', 'Because GitHub requires it'], a: 0, why: 'By default, a step after a failed step is skipped. always() makes it run anyway.' },
  { type: 'order', q: 'Put the CI steps in order.', items: ['Check out the code', 'Set up the language', 'Install the libraries', 'Install the browsers with their system libraries', 'Run the tests', 'Upload the report and traces'], why: 'Each step needs the one before it. The upload is last, and it runs even after a failure.' },
  { q: 'Why does a fresh Linux CI machine need the "--with-deps" option when you install the browsers?', o: ['Browsers need system libraries that a clean machine does not have', 'It makes the browser smaller', 'It installs the tests', 'It is required only for Firefox on Windows'], a: 0, why: 'A browser is a real program. A clean server lacks fonts and graphics libraries that it needs to start.' }
],
practice: { id: 'p-m11', title: 'A suite that runs itself',
  brief: '<p>For your project: (1) turn one of your tests into a parameterized test with at least three inputs; (2) move the address of the site into a setting (the base address); (3) run the suite in parallel, and note the time before and after; (4) add the CI workflow, push it to a repository on GitHub, and make the first run green; (5) make one test fail on purpose in CI, download the artifact, and open the trace. Write down the times and what you saw.</p>',
  template: 'Time without parallel runs:\nTime with parallel runs:\n\nThe CI run (link or description):\n\nWhat the trace of the failed test showed:\n',
  checks: ['The parameterized test reports each input separately', 'The address of the site is a setting, not repeated in the tests', 'Parallel runs pass, and I wrote down both times', 'The CI job is green on a clean machine', 'I downloaded the artifact of a failed run and opened its trace'],
  model: '<p><b>A typical result:</b> 17 tests took about 11 seconds in one process and about 4 seconds with four workers. The CI run installs the browser, runs the tests and, for the deliberate failure, uploads <code>test-results/</code> (Python) or <code>build/reports</code> and <code>build/traces</code> (Kotlin) as an artifact. Opening the trace shows the red step and the page at that moment.</p><p><b>If the parallel run fails</b> while the serial run passes, do not turn parallel runs off: find the test that shares something (a user, a file, an order), and make it independent.</p>' }
});

